/**

@page page_exahype_tracer Tracers

\tableofcontents

ExaHyPE tracers are particle sets one solver moves through its solution. A
project declares the set, inserts its particles, hands the set to a solver
and can write the particle histories to CSV files.

# Adding a tracer set

The project creates a set with four data entries per particle:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tracers = project.add_tracer(name="MyTracer", particle_attributes=4)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The insertions run once at the start of the grid initialisation on the rank
whose mesh holds the position:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tracers.insert_by_coordinates([[x0, y0], [x1, y1]])
tracers.insert_with_data([[x0, y0, q0, q1, q2, q3]])
tracers.insert_from_file("particles.dat", scale_factor=1.0)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

A coordinate carries at least one component per axis of the build. The
argument `transform` of `insert_by_coordinates` names a C++ callable of the
solver that maps every coordinate onto the position the particle takes; the
curvilinear ExaSeis applications pass `invertProjection` to place a tracer at
the mesh position of a physical coordinate.

# Moving the tracers

A solver moves a set after the grid initialisation and after every time step
the two flags select, with the forces the set carries:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver.trace(
  tracers,
  exahype2.particles.Forces(
    interpolation_scheme=2,
    integration_scheme=0,
    force_method=0,
    fields_to_interpolate="0,1,2,3",
  ),
  initialisation=False,
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

`fields_to_interpolate` lists the data entries that receive the first
unknowns of the solver in this order. The interpolation scheme 0 reads no
solver value, 1 reads the volume that holds the position and 2 interpolates
between the volumes; a DG solver family evaluates its nodal polynomial for
every scheme other than 0. The integration scheme 0 keeps the particle in
place, and the schemes 1 to 6 advance it with the interpolated velocity and
the forces of `force_method`. A finite-volume time step moves the tracers
through the solution it read, so the tracers and the fluid advance from the
same state.

The set lives in exahype2::particles::ParticleSet: the position and the data
of every particle in one array, the two-integer number of the particle and
the slot of the cell that holds it. A particle that left its rank travels to
the rank whose partition holds its new position after every trace.

# Sampling the traces of a plane

A set can record the face traces of a DG solver family on one plane, such as
the fault of a dynamic rupture application:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
fault = project.add_tracer(name="FaultTracer", particle_attributes=12)
fault.insert_by_coordinates(coordinates, transform="invertProjection")
fault.sample_face_traces(
  normal="faultNormal", position="faultPosition", difference_fields=["u", "v"]
)
solver.trace(fault, initialisation=False)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Every particle on the plane takes the mean of the two traces of every stored
unknown, and for the unknowns of `difference_fields` the trace of the upper
cell minus the trace of the lower cell. `normal` and `position` are C++
expressions the generated solver evaluates.

# Writing trajectories

`dump_into_database` appends a snapshot of every particle to a CSV file after
every trace, once the particle moved further than the position delta or more
time than the time delta passed, within the closed record window:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
tracers.dump_into_database(
  filename="Tracer",
  position_delta_between_two_snapshots=1.0e16,
  time_delta_between_two_snapshots=0.01,
  output_precision=10,
)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

Each row contains the particle number, the time stamp, the coordinates and
the data entries. The particle number combines the rank of insertion with a
rank-local counter, so a particle keeps its identity after it migrates. An MPI
run appends the records of each rank to `<filename>-rank-<rank>.csv`; a serial
run writes `<filename>.csv`. `postprocessing/plot_probe_data.py` reads one
CSV file and groups its rows by particle number.

# Measuring the position error

A solver with the `particle_analytical_solution` PDE term records the L1, L2
and Linf errors of the particle positions against it:

~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
solver.measure_particle_error(tracers, file="ParticleError", every=0.01)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

*/
